            {
                id infra.tx-review
                title "Review the transaction and connection execution API"
                kind @chore
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:25:19.333895Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Todo → Doing (nplan start)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:29:24.461286Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @decision
                        text "Review conclusions (full report delivered to operator): (1) expose awaitable rollback() — pgorm-pool already has it; commit/rollback both take() the Option so Drop's warn automatically becomes implicit-path-only. (2) Isolation/access config via public inherent DatabaseConnection::begin_with(TransactionOptions{isolation_level, read_only, deferrable}) — NOT on TransactionTrait (savepoints cannot take isolation); delete the dead private begin_with_config. (3) Do NOT revive the closure transaction API — sea-orm's shape relied on an owned Arc<Mutex> handle; over tokio_postgres Transaction<'a> it forces double-lifetime HRTBs; Drop-rollback + explicit API covers it. (4) Add batch_execute as a sixth ConnectionTrait method (simple-query protocol, bypasses statement cache by nature, must be visible to the metric layer). (5) Fire-and-forget Drop rollback is ordered-safe (pipelining + &mut borrow + surviving driver task) — no spawn-await workaround; document the argument in conn.tx.guard. (6) begin(&mut self) is forced by tokio-postgres's borrow and is the anti-interleaving feature; add InstrumentedConnection::begin_instrumented for the metric gap. (7) Pool parallelism is a test-coverage item only — no get_many helper. Decomposed into infra.tx.{rollback,config,batch,metric-tx,tests}."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:29:29.481195Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-01T21:35:41.268131Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @errata
                        text "Recommendation 3 (do not revive the closure transaction API) was wrong — it was calibrated against the pre-1.85 BoxFuture encoding. Operator challenged it; an empirical spike compiled and ran clean against live Postgres on stable (MSRV 1.85, already this repo's floor). Two things dissolve the double-lifetime objection: (a) AsyncFnOnce natively lets the returned future borrow the &mut txn argument, so the for<'c> rank is carried by the trait machinery with no boxing; (b) the transaction's own lifetime need not be higher-ranked — take &'s mut DatabaseConnection and MOVE that borrow into begin (TransactionTrait::begin(conn), not a reborrow), so the closure sees the concrete DatabaseTransaction<'s>. Working signature: async fn transaction<'s, F, T, E>(conn: &'s mut DatabaseConnection, f: F) -> Result<T, TransactionError<E>> where F: AsyncFnOnce(&mut DatabaseTransaction<'s>) -> Result<T, E>. Closure API is feasible; adding it is now a pure API-surface judgment (sugar: auto-commit on Ok + TransactionError wrapping; Drop already covers rollback-on-error). It also unlocks a retry-on-40001 serializable-retry helper via AsyncFnMut, which the explicit API cannot express."
                        after_done true
                    }
                )
            }

