            {
                id ergo.fluent-pipeline
                title "The pipeline reads like the PRQL: columns are bare, closures only where binding, arrays not vecs"
                kind @refactor
                deps (ergo.pipeline-v1)
                sealed true
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-04T15:00:08.718176Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Operator directive (2026-09-04): the pipeline's correctness is right but the surface is line noise, not a fluent API. Six mechanical noise sources with agreed directions: (1) bare columns — ColumnTrait carries its entity, so O::Total converts to a qualified pipeline Expr directly (IntoExpr impl); two-arg col() remains for disambiguation only; (2) closures only where binding — value-taking transforms (.sort([..]), .select([..]), .filter(expr)) with _with(|b| ..) variants for parameter-bearing forms; (3) impl IntoIterator everywhere a Vec is taken, so arrays replace vec!; (4) aggregate_by's (vec, vec) tuple dies — .group([keys]).aggregate([aggs]) two-step reading like the PRQL, with the typestate judgment on aggregate-after-group; (5) aliased -> as_ (rename outright, delete-don't-deprecate); (6) comparisons/functions generic over Into<Expr> with Rust literal impls (i64/f64/&str/bool) INLINED as literals exactly like literals in PRQL text — the Binder stays the explicit, only door for runtime values, and the brand invariance guarantee must survive untouched. Target shape shown to operator (top-2-per-customer in 4 lines: window(row_number().as_(\"rn\"), by(O::CustomerId), sort(O::Total.desc())) / filter(out(\"rn\").lte(2)) / select([O::CustomerId, O::Total, out(\"rn\")])) — the exact combinator spellings (by/sort helpers vs WindowDef builder) are implementer taste within that budget. Also consider entity-side entry sugar (order::query() or Entity::pipeline()). All existing tests/doctests churn to the new surface; compile_fail proofs (brand smuggling) must keep failing for the right reason."
                    }

