                        kind @note
                        text "Verification defect on fa0aa01b, 2026-09-07. Review severity P1: silent incorrect decoded values (not a planning priority pin). Source: src/executor/result_name.rs:28-36. Plain aliases pass through when shorter than 63 bytes, but trimming the bounded alias head to a UTF-8 boundary can produce a 60-, 61-, or 62-byte alias. These namespaces therefore still overlap. Reproduction: A = 'a'.repeat(43) + 'å' + 'b'.repeat(16), B = 'a'.repeat(43) + 'd9004b29644599f8'; both are valid, distinct PostgreSQL column identifiers, respectively 61 and 59 bytes. Under s0_, A's full composition is 64 bytes and its hash spelling retreats to a 46-byte head plus 16 hash digits, for 62 total bytes. B's 62-byte composition is identical and passes through unchanged. Store left='left', right='right': ordinary find decodes correctly, graph yields ('left','left'), and Pipeline::select_sources yields ('right','right'). This is deterministic alias-versus-plain overlap, independent of the accepted theoretical 64-bit hash-collision risk. Acceptance: bounded/plain alias namespaces must remain disjoint for every possible UTF-8 head boundary, including 2-, 3-, and 4-byte characters and multiple source prefixes; preserve the 63-byte maximum, consistent writer/read/absence mapping, and all original regression probes. Add live regression coverage that checks distinct values through graph and pipeline for the concrete pair, plus boundary coverage of every possible UTF-8 retreat length. Spec impact: correct query.graph.writer+3's false claim that every plain spelling is shorter than every bounded spelling, and reconcile its references after revision. Depends on the completed ASCII collision fix; manual follow-up leaf avoids duplicate spec ownership. Command: cargo nextest run --workspace --no-fail-fast, with /tmp/pgorm-defect-recheck-fa0aa01b-repros.patch applied: 1,351 tests run, 1,350 passed, one failed (unicode_bounded_alias_does_not_collide_with_short_column), zero skipped. Output: /tmp/pgorm-defect-recheck-fa0aa01b.log."
